1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,550
September, nineteen forty. German bombers
come back to London every single night.

2
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:09,551
And every evening, thousands of
families walk down into the Tube,

3
00:00:09,571 --> 00:00:12,010
carrying blankets, bread
and their children.

4
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:15,289
The government had banned
it. So how do you eat,

5
00:00:15,309 --> 00:00:18,630
sleep and survive under a burning
city for fifty-seven nights?

6
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:23,507
In nineteen forty, London already
had the perfect bomb shelter:

7
00:00:23,527 --> 00:00:26,830
miles of deep railway
tunnels under its streets.

8
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:30,350
Some platforms sat more than
twenty metres below the pavement.

9
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:32,710
But the government said no.

10
00:00:33,340 --> 00:00:36,558
The Tube had to keep moving
workers, soldiers and supplies,

11
00:00:36,578 --> 00:00:38,770
so the platforms had to stay clear.

12
00:00:39,740 --> 00:00:42,772
Officials also feared that
frightened people would go down and

13
00:00:42,792 --> 00:00:44,350
simply never come back up.

14
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:48,450
So Londoners found a loophole, and
it cost them one and a half pence.

15
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:51,964
They bought the cheapest ticket,
walked down to the platform,

16
00:00:51,984 --> 00:00:53,350
and refused to leave.

17
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:56,861
Station staff could not
throw out thousands of paying

18
00:00:56,881 --> 00:00:58,510
passengers, night after night.

19
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:03,321
In the first nights of the Blitz, crowds
at Liverpool Street station pushed in as

20
00:01:03,341 --> 00:01:04,950
soon as the sirens started.

21
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:07,829
The rule from the top said no shelterers.

22
00:01:07,849 --> 00:01:10,050
The crowd on the stairs said otherwise.

23
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:13,749
Within weeks, the government
gave in, and the stations were

24
00:01:13,769 --> 00:01:15,450
officially opened as shelters.

25
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:18,358
On the night of September twenty-seventh,

26
00:01:18,378 --> 00:01:21,850
one hundred seventy-seven
thousand people slept underground.

27
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:24,924
But now the Tube had
a strange new problem:

28
00:01:24,944 --> 00:01:27,250
where do you put a small city every night?

29
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:29,430
The first answer was paint.

30
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:32,460
Staff painted white lines
along the platforms,

31
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:34,490
because the trains were still running.

32
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:38,528
Until half past seven in the
evening, shelterers had to stay eight

33
00:01:38,548 --> 00:01:40,010
feet back from the edge.

34
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:44,330
After eight o'clock, they could move
up to just four feet from the trains.

35
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:47,262
Around half past ten,
the last trains stopped,

36
00:01:47,282 --> 00:01:50,650
the lights were dimmed, and
the power in the rails was cut.

37
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:53,858
Then people slept wherever they could:

38
00:01:53,878 --> 00:01:58,510
on platforms, on stairs, in passages,
even on escalators that had stopped.

39
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:01,451
Nobody had pillows.
Most people had a coat,

40
00:02:01,471 --> 00:02:03,410
a blanket and the cold concrete.

41
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:08,312
The best spots went fast. Some families
queued from ten in the morning to

42
00:02:08,332 --> 00:02:09,890
claim a patch of floor.

43
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:14,949
Soon, a black market appeared: a good
place on a platform could be sold for two

44
00:02:14,969 --> 00:02:16,590
shillings and sixpence.

45
00:02:17,480 --> 00:02:20,217
So the authorities brought
in a new kind of ticket:

46
00:02:20,237 --> 00:02:22,290
not for travel, but for sleeping.

47
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:24,818
Regular families got a reserved place.

48
00:02:24,838 --> 00:02:28,910
About one spot in ten stayed free
for strangers caught in a raid.

49
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:34,010
One short branch line, to Aldwych, was
closed to trains completely and handed

50
00:02:34,030 --> 00:02:35,370
over to shelterers.

51
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:38,389
People could now sleep
on the tracks themselves,

52
00:02:38,409 --> 00:02:40,470
because no train would ever come.

53
00:02:41,220 --> 00:02:44,025
Then came the bunks:
twenty-four thousand of them,

54
00:02:44,045 --> 00:02:46,510
fixed along platform walls and tunnels.

55
00:02:46,910 --> 00:02:50,710
Laid end to end, those bunks would
have stretched for twenty-seven miles.

56
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:55,789
So sleep was solved, more or less. But
more than a hundred thousand tired people

57
00:02:55,809 --> 00:02:57,130
still needed to eat.

58
00:02:58,020 --> 00:03:00,241
At first, families brought their own food:

59
00:03:00,261 --> 00:03:03,430
sandwiches, cold tea in a
flask, maybe a slice of cake.

60
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:08,690
And Britain was already on rationing, so
every slice of bacon and every spoon of

61
00:03:08,710 --> 00:03:09,990
sugar was counted.

62
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:14,450
So London Transport turned its
own trains into delivery vans.

63
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:17,370
They were called Tube
Refreshment Specials,

64
00:03:17,390 --> 00:03:19,730
and they left their
depots every afternoon.

65
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:24,848
By December nineteen forty, one report
said these trains carried seven tons

66
00:03:24,868 --> 00:03:26,270
of food every night.

67
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:28,982
That meant around thirty thousand buns,

68
00:03:29,002 --> 00:03:31,090
plus endless cups of tea and cocoa.

69
00:03:31,740 --> 00:03:35,341
By the end of the war, London
Transport had served over half a

70
00:03:35,361 --> 00:03:36,990
million gallons of tea.

71
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:41,755
Staff and volunteers served it from
more than a hundred and thirty canteen

72
00:03:41,775 --> 00:03:43,230
points on the platforms.

73
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:46,498
But food goes in, and it has to come out.

74
00:03:46,518 --> 00:03:49,450
And that was the problem
nobody had planned for.

75
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:53,248
Tube stations were built for
passengers who stayed a few minutes,

76
00:03:53,268 --> 00:03:55,390
not for families who stayed all night.

77
00:03:55,820 --> 00:03:59,604
At Holborn, a station that could hold
four thousand six hundred people,

78
00:03:59,624 --> 00:04:01,670
there were four chemical toilets.

79
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:03,850
And four buckets.

80
00:04:04,340 --> 00:04:08,642
The air grew hot, heavy and smelly, and
doctors feared that one sick person could

81
00:04:08,662 --> 00:04:10,230
infect a whole platform.

82
00:04:10,630 --> 00:04:12,810
Lice spread from blanket to blanket.

83
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:17,254
And in the warm tunnels,
shelterers complained about another

84
00:04:17,274 --> 00:04:18,380
enemy: mosquitoes.

85
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:21,448
By early nineteen forty-one,
staff were spraying stations,

86
00:04:21,468 --> 00:04:23,590
careful to stop while people slept.

87
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:27,003
Slowly, things got better,
with proper toilets,

88
00:04:27,023 --> 00:04:30,450
first aid posts and nurses
on duty at the big shelters.

89
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:33,852
But if the Tube was
crowded, dirty and smelly,

90
00:04:33,872 --> 00:04:36,870
why did families keep coming
back, night after night?

91
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:41,974
Because down there, it was quieter. The
bombs above sounded like distant thuds

92
00:04:41,994 --> 00:04:43,890
instead of screaming whistles.

93
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:48,630
At the peak, around twenty-five thousand
children spent the night in the stations.

94
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:52,965
They played, did their homework,
and slept between strangers who soon

95
00:04:52,985 --> 00:04:54,290
became neighbours.

96
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:57,318
Some shelters got lending
libraries, concerts,

97
00:04:57,338 --> 00:04:59,510
film shows and even evening lectures.

98
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:04,730
For many families, the platform stopped
being a shelter and became a kind of home.

99
00:05:05,820 --> 00:05:08,890
Picture a typical evening for a
mother in the East End of London.

100
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:11,776
She finishes work, collects the children,

101
00:05:11,796 --> 00:05:13,922
grabs the blankets and the shelter ticket,

102
00:05:13,942 --> 00:05:15,490
and walks to the station.

103
00:05:15,980 --> 00:05:19,850
In the morning, she climbs back up to
see if her house is still standing.

104
00:05:20,740 --> 00:05:24,629
Over the whole war, London Transport
counted about sixty-three million

105
00:05:24,649 --> 00:05:26,110
visits by shelterers.

106
00:05:26,700 --> 00:05:30,706
Underground, people felt safe. But
they were about to learn that the Tube

107
00:05:30,726 --> 00:05:32,010
was not bombproof.

108
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:36,745
The first warning came in September, when
a bomb hit the subway at Marble Arch and

109
00:05:36,765 --> 00:05:38,130
killed twenty people.

110
00:05:38,980 --> 00:05:42,110
In October, Trafalgar Square
and Bounds Green were hit too.

111
00:05:42,520 --> 00:05:44,759
On October fourteenth, nineteen forty,

112
00:05:44,779 --> 00:05:47,810
a heavy bomb hit the road
above Balham station.

113
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:52,570
The crater was so big that a
double-decker bus drove straight into it.

114
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:56,519
Water mains and sewers burst, and
the tunnel below began to fill

115
00:05:56,539 --> 00:05:57,970
with water and gravel.

116
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:01,684
Sixty-eight people died that
night, most of them shelterers,

117
00:06:01,704 --> 00:06:03,250
four of them railway staff.

118
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:07,190
Three months later, a bomb fell
through the road onto the booking

119
00:06:07,210 --> 00:06:08,490
hall at Bank station.

120
00:06:08,940 --> 00:06:13,213
More than fifty people were killed, and
the crater was so large the army built

121
00:06:13,233 --> 00:06:14,570
a bridge across it.

122
00:06:15,380 --> 00:06:17,470
So the government finally ordered new,

123
00:06:17,490 --> 00:06:20,770
deeper shelters, dug far
below the existing Tube lines.

124
00:06:21,720 --> 00:06:24,865
Eight of them were finished. But
they were not ready until the worst

125
00:06:24,885 --> 00:06:26,190
of the Blitz was over.

126
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:30,935
So for those fifty-seven nights,
Londoners trusted old railway tunnels,

127
00:06:30,955 --> 00:06:32,550
painted lines, and luck.

128
00:06:33,300 --> 00:06:37,270
Which leaves the real question: how
much did the Tube actually save London?

129
00:06:37,680 --> 00:06:41,612
In November nineteen forty, officials
counted where Londoners were really

130
00:06:41,632 --> 00:06:43,110
spending their nights.

131
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:48,530
The result surprised them. Only about
four percent were sheltering in the Tube.

132
00:06:49,220 --> 00:06:51,508
Nine percent used big public shelters,

133
00:06:51,528 --> 00:06:55,210
and twenty-seven percent slept
in small shelters at home.

134
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:00,030
The rest, more than half of London, simply
stayed in their own houses and hoped.

135
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:03,585
So the Tube did not save
London by being big.

136
00:07:03,605 --> 00:07:06,970
It saved the people inside
it by being organised.

137
00:07:07,700 --> 00:07:10,958
Tickets, painted lines, food
trains, and staff who kept a hundred

138
00:07:10,978 --> 00:07:12,470
thousand strangers calm.

139
00:07:13,360 --> 00:07:15,512
The proof came in March
nineteen forty-three,

140
00:07:15,532 --> 00:07:17,010
at Bethnal Green station.

141
00:07:17,980 --> 00:07:21,946
During an alert, a new anti-aircraft
rocket battery nearby fired,

142
00:07:21,966 --> 00:07:24,390
with a sound few people recognised.

143
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:28,510
Crowds hurried down the dark stairs,
and a woman near the bottom fell.

144
00:07:28,880 --> 00:07:31,555
In seconds, people piled
on top of each other.

145
00:07:31,575 --> 00:07:35,610
One hundred seventy-three died,
and no bomb ever hit the station.

146
00:07:36,760 --> 00:07:39,443
That was the real lesson
of the Tube. Underground,

147
00:07:39,463 --> 00:07:42,470
the biggest danger was not
the bombs. It was panic.

148
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:46,414
The government kept the Bethnal
Green report quiet until the

149
00:07:46,434 --> 00:07:47,770
war was nearly over.

150
00:07:48,660 --> 00:07:50,889
And on the sixth of May,
nineteen forty-five,

151
00:07:50,909 --> 00:07:53,724
the last three hundred forty-four
shelterers climbed back

152
00:07:53,744 --> 00:07:55,070
up into the daylight.

153
00:07:55,820 --> 00:08:00,190
So how did Londoners eat, sleep and
survive fifty-seven nights underground?

154
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:04,081
With a cheap ticket, a painted
line and tea from a train.

155
00:08:04,101 --> 00:08:06,590
And above all, by staying calm in a crowd.

156
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:10,182
But what about the people
who never went underground?

157
00:08:10,202 --> 00:08:13,451
Next, we open the tin shelter
in the back garden that millions

158
00:08:13,471 --> 00:08:15,070
trusted with their lives.

159
00:08:15,980 --> 00:08:17,370
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
